<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · War and march · 1847 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Il Canto degli italiani</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song written by a young poet in Genoa in 1847 to call Italians to unite; since 1946 it has been Italy's national anthem.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Goffredo Mameli</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Michele Novaro</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Italian patriots of the Risorgimento; later the Italian Republic</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Genoa and Turin, Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words September 1847; music 24 November 1847; first public performance 10 December 1847</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Goffredo Mameli and music by Michele Novaro (1847); both authors died more than 70 years ago)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>Goffredo Mameli, a young patriot from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> with republican and Jacobin sympathies, wrote the words in September 1847 (sources give the 8th or the 10th), inspired by the mass mobilisations that would lead to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">revolutions of 1848</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-italian-war-of-independence">First Italian War of Independence</a>. On 10 November he sent the text to the Genoese composer Michele Novaro in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, who set it to music on 24 November. The text drew on &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a>&quot;, and Mameli changed its original opening, probably at Novaro's suggestion; a verse addressed to Italian women was dropped before the debut. Italians also call it the &quot;Inno di Mameli&quot; after its author, or &quot;Fratelli d'Italia&quot;.</p><p>Its first known public performance was on 10 December 1847, at a demonstration before the sanctuary of Our Lady of Loreto in Oregina, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. Officially it marked the 101st anniversary of the Portoria quarter's revolt that drove the Austrians from the city; in fact it was a protest against foreign occupation and a call on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-albert-of-sardinia">Charles Albert of Sardinia</a> to take up the Italian cause. The municipal band of Sestri Ponente played the song for some 30,000 patriots, and an earlier performance on 9 November may have taken place. Because Mameli was a known follower of Mazzini, the Piedmontese police banned the song until March 1848, and the Austrian police treated singing it as a political crime until the end of their empire.</p><p>In 1848 the song spread by word of mouth across the peninsula. It was sung in the Five Days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, by soldiers and military bands in the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> (King Charles Albert had to withdraw his censorship of its fifth strophe), and by the volunteers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a> hummed it during the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Garibaldi's volunteers sang it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand">Expedition of the Thousand</a> in 1860, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Giuseppe Verdi</a> chose it to stand for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in his &quot;Hymn of the Nations&quot; for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> International Exhibition of 1862. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> was proclaimed in 1861, however, its republican content did not suit the monarchy, which kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>'s &quot;Marcia Reale&quot;. The song returned to favour in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italo-turkish-war">Italo-Turkish War</a> and the trenches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>.</p><p>Under fascism it was discouraged and kept out of official ceremonies, though tolerated on some occasions. After the armistice of 8 September 1943 it was sung in the liberated south and in partisan areas, and it was popular in anti-fascist circles; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s Social Republic also played it, so it was sung by partisans and fascists alike. On 12 October 1946, on the proposal of War Minister Cipriano Facchinetti, the government of the new Republic adopted it as the provisional national anthem. Attempts to make this permanent stalled for decades, until President Carlo Azeglio Ciampi revived it as a national symbol (1999–2006) and a law of 4 December 2017 made it the official anthem.</p><p>On official occasions the first two stanzas are sung without the introduction; the sixth stanza is almost never performed. In 2025 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergio-mattarella">Sergio Mattarella</a> signed a decree that the shout of assent Novaro had added at the end of the refrain, which was not in Mameli's text, is no longer to be sung. Novaro took no payment for the music, asking that a publisher's fee go to a subscription for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A call to the Italians, divided for centuries, to unite and rise to fight for their freedom, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> once did. Its strophes appeal to God to protect their union, recall heroes and moments of past struggles against foreign rule, and name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> as the enemy; the mood is urgent and martial, quickening to a rousing refrain.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Italian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Italian</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Fratelli d'Italia,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Brothers of Italy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">l'Italia s'è desta,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Italy has awoken,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">dell'elmo di Scipio</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with the helmet of Scipio</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">s'è cinta la testa.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>she has girded her head.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Dov'è la Vittoria?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Where is Victory?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Le porga la chioma,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let her offer her hair,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">ché schiava di Roma</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for as the slave of Rome</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Iddio la creò.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God created her.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Stringiamci a coorte,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us close ranks in a cohort,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">siam pronti alla morte.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we are ready for death.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Siam pronti alla morte,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We are ready for death,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">l'Italia chiamò.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Italy has called.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Noi fummo da secoli</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We have for centuries been</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">calpesti, derisi,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>trampled, mocked,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">perché non siam popolo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>because we are not a people,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">perché siam divisi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>because we are divided.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Raccolgaci un'unica</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let a single</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">bandiera, una speme:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>flag, one hope, gather us:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">di fonderci insieme</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for us to merge together</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">già l'ora suonò.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the hour has already struck.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 44 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Uniamoci, amiamoci,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us unite, let us love one another;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">l'unione e l'amore</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>union and love</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">rivelano ai popoli</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>reveal to the peoples</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">le vie del Signore.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the ways of the Lord.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Giuriamo far libero</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us swear to make free</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">il suolo natio:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the native soil:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">uniti, per Dio,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>united, by God,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">chi vincer ci può?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who can defeat us?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Dall'Alpi a Sicilia</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From the Alps to Sicily</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">dovunque è Legnano,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>everywhere is Legnano;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">ogn'uom di Ferruccio</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>every man has Ferruccio's</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">ha il core, ha la mano,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>heart, has his hand;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">i bimbi d'Italia</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the children of Italy</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">si chiaman Balilla,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>are called Balilla;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">il suon d'ogni squilla</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the sound of every bell</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">i Vespri suonò.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>has rung the Vespers.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Son giunchi che piegano</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>They are rushes that bend,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">le spade vendute:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the swords that are sold:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">già l'Aquila d'Austria</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>already the Eagle of Austria</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">le penne ha perdute.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>has lost its feathers.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">Il sangue d'Italia,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The blood of Italy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">il sangue Polacco,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Polish blood,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">bevé, col cosacco,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it drank, with the Cossack,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="it">ma il cor le bruciò.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>but it burned its heart.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Goffredo Mameli (1847); the text as sung since 1946, as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canto_degli_italiani" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (9)</summary><ul><li>line 1.3: Scipio Africanus, the Roman general who defeated Hannibal at Zama.</li><li>lines 1.5-1.8: Victory is to offer her hair to Italy (to be cut, as a slave's was), for God made her Rome's slave.</li><li>line 2.1: 'Stringiamci a coorte': close up as a cohort, the Roman legion's unit.</li><li>line 5.2: Legnano, where the Lombard League defeated Frederick Barbarossa (1176).</li><li>lines 5.3-5.4: literally 'every man of Ferruccio / has the heart, has the hand': every man has the heart and hand of Francesco Ferruccio, defender of the Florentine Republic (1530).</li><li>line 5.6: Balilla, the boy who started the Genoese revolt against the Austrians (1746): every Italian child is a Balilla.</li><li>line 5.8: the Sicilian Vespers, the rising against the Angevins (1282), begun at the bell for vespers.</li><li>lines 6.1-6.2: the mercenary swords (of Austria's hired troops) are rushes that bend.</li><li>lines 6.7-6.8: the Austrian Eagle drank Italian and Polish blood together with the Cossack (Russia), but that blood burned its heart.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
